.TH tuarchitecture n 0.1 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuarchitecture
.PP
Parses a Mermaid \fBarchitecture\-beta\fR block into a \fBtclutils::tudiagram\fR model so an architecture diagram renders natively (SVG or PNG) through the pure\-Tcl engine everywhere \-\- no browser. Like \fBtuc4\fR, this is a \fBgraph parser\fR: it only builds a model; layout and rendering are done by \fBtudiagram\fR. Normally you call \fBtuflow::toSvg\fR / \fBtuflow::toPng\fR, which detect the \fBarchitecture\-beta\fR header, parse here, and lay the graph out.
.SS Package
.CS
package require tclutils::tuarchitecture 0.1
.CE
.SS Commands
.CS
::tclutils::tuarchitecture::parse text   ;# -> tudiagram model dict
.CE
.PP
Render the returned model with the tudiagram backends:
.CS
::tclutils::tudiagram::toSvg  model
::tclutils::tudiagram::toPng   model ?-scale n?
::tclutils::tudiagram::writeSvg model file
::tclutils::tudiagram::writePng model file
.CE
.SS "Supported syntax (Mermaid subset)"
.CS
architecture-beta
  group <id>(<icon>)[<Label>]            -> a group, drawn as a cluster frame
  service <id>(<icon>)[<Label>] in <g>   -> a service node (`in <group>` optional)
  junction <id>                          -> a routing point (drawn as a dot)
  <id>:<S> -- <S>:<id>                    -> edge, no arrowhead
  <id>:<S> --> <S>:<id>                   -> edge, arrowhead at the target
.CE
.IP \(bu
The header is \fBarchitecture\-beta\fR (bare \fBarchitecture\fR is also accepted).
.IP \(bu
The display label is the \fB[Label]\fR, or the id if absent.
.IP \(bu
\fB<S>\fR is a side hint \fBL|R|T|B\fR; \fB\-\-\fR / \fB\-\->\fR / \fB<\-\-\fR / \fB<\-\->\fR choose the arrowheads (none / end / start / both).
.IP \(bu
Edge endpoints that were never declared are created as plain boxes, so an edge to an as\-yet\-undeclared id still renders.
.IP \(bu
Blank lines and \fB%%\fR comments are ignored.
.SS "Icons \-> shapes"
.PP
There is no external icon set; the \fB(icon)\fR maps to a tudiagram node shape so families stay readable:
.TS
l l.
icon	shape
\fBdatabase\fR, \fBdb\fR, \fBdisk\fR, \fBstorage\fR	cylinder
\fBcloud\fR, \fBinternet\fR	rounded
\fBserver\fR and anything else	box
.TE
.SS "v1 limitations (honest)"
.IP \(bu
\fBGroups draw as cluster frames\fR (needs \fBtudiagram\fR >= 0.4). A \fBgroup\fR becomes a labelled box around the bounding box of its \fBin <group>\fR members; nested \fBgroup ... in <parent>\fR is honoured (members roll up). The frame is a post\-hoc bounding box over the laid\-out members, so it is tight when a group is a connected sub\-cluster and looser when the layout scatters them. A group id that an edge references is drawn as a plain node.
.IP \(bu
\fBSide hints are not routed.\fR \fB:L :R :T :B\fR are parsed but do not steer the edge ports \-\- the engine decides port positions (the same limitation as \fBtuc4\fR's \fBRel_U/D/L/R\fR hints). The declared topology survives; the exact approach side may differ from Mermaid.
.IP \(bu
\fBIcons are shapes, not glyphs\fR (no Iconify dependency).
.SS "mermaid.js compatibility (read this before using a \fBmermaid\fR fence)"
.PP
There are \fBtwo independent parsers\fR for the same fence content. A raster sink (PDF, ODT, Tk, or a \fBflow\fR fence) renders through \fBtuflow \-> tuarchitecture\fR. An HTML export with \fBenableMermaid 1\fR hands a \fBmermaid\fR block to \fBmermaid.js\fR in the browser instead. \fBtuarchitecture\fR accepts a \fBlenient superset\fR of mermaid.js \fBarchitecture\-beta\fR, so a block that renders here can still throw \fBSyntax error in text\fR in a browser running mermaid.js 11.x.
.PP
Everything in the \fBofficial mermaid examples renders identically here\fR (verified against \fBtuflow::toSvg\fR/\fBtoPng\fR): write that subset and a \fBmermaid\fR block works in \fBboth\fR paths.
.PP
Safe\-subset checklist (renders in mermaid.js \fBand\fR tuflow):
.IP \(bu
Header \fBarchitecture\-beta\fR.
.IP \(bu
\fBgroup id(icon)[Label]\fR and \fBservice id(icon)[Label]\fR, optional \fBin <group>\fR (groups may nest: \fBgroup inner(icon)[..] in outer\fR).
.IP \(bu
Built\-in icons only: \fBcloud\fR, \fBdatabase\fR, \fBdisk\fR, \fBinternet\fR, \fBserver\fR (or a registered iconify pack like \fBlogos:aws\-lambda\fR).
.IP \(bu
Edges \fBa:S \-\- S:b\fR with sides \fBT|B|L|R\fR; arrowheads \fB\-\->\fR, \fB<\-\-\fR, \fB<\-\->\fR. The cross\-group modifier \fBa{group}:S \-\-> S:b{group}\fR is tolerated here (the modifier is stripped; the edge still connects).
.IP \(bu
\fBDeclare every id before an edge references it.\fR
.IP \(bu
Keep ids to plain \fB[A\-Za\-z0\-9_]\fR; prefer simple, single\-word labels. Labels with spaces appear in the official examples (\fB[Cloud Infrastructure]\fR); other characters (hyphens, slashes, umlauts) are \fBnot guaranteed\fR by mermaid.js and are a common cause of its "Syntax error".
.PP
If you need an architecture diagram in HTML that does \fBnot\fR depend on mermaid.js, use a \fBflow\fR fence: docir then renders inline SVG via \fBtuflow\fR, identical to the PDF output. (A docir\-side alternative is to treat \fBarchitecture\-beta\fR as native when \fBtuflow::toSvg\fR succeeds, instead of delegating to mermaid.js.)
.CS
package require tclutils::tuarchitecture
set src {architecture-beta
    group tier(cloud)[Application Tier]
    service web(server)[Web UI] in tier
    service api(server)[API] in tier
    service db(database)[Database] in tier
    web:R -- L:api
    api:B -- T:db}
::tclutils::tudiagram::writePng [::tclutils::tuarchitecture::parse $src] arch.png -scale 3
.CE
.PP
In Markdown, \fBarchitecture\-beta\fR rides on the \fB```mermaid\fR fence, so docir's raster sinks (pdf / odt / rendererTk) render it through \fBtuflow::toPng\fR; no docir change is needed.
.SS Notes
.IP \(bu
Companion: \fBtclutils::tudiagram\fR (layout + render), \fBtclutils::tuflow\fR (dispatch). \fBtuflow::parse\fR delegates \fBarchitecture\-beta\fR here and returns the tudiagram model, exactly like \fBmindmap\fR / \fBC4*\fR / \fBgitGraph\fR.
.IP \(bu
Because it is a graph type, the same model also flows through every other tudiagram consumer unchanged.
.SS "Error codes"
.PP
\fB\-errorcode {TCLUTILS TUARCHITECTURE <REASON>}\fR \-\- \fBEMPTY\fR (no services; a diagram consisting only of empty groups also yields no nodes).
